<xml>
  <block type="st_sd" x="25" y="25">
    <mutation uid="image_card" />
    <field name="NAME">card</field>
    <field name="CS">10</field>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Purpose: Store 1024 brightness levels in a 16-bit PGM file, avoiding BMP palette limits.
Hardware: use Connections to check pin ownership for your selected board. Open the comment bubbles on the main steps for explanations.</comment>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_typed_variable" x="310" y="25">
    <field name="TYPE">uint32_t</field>
    <field name="VAR">levels</field>
    <value name="VALUE">
      <block type="math_number">
        <field name="NUM">1024</field>
      </block>
    </value>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Create levels as uint32_t. Outside a body it is global; inside a function/setup/loop it belongs to that body. Its value input is the initial value.</comment>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_setup_section" x="25" y="100">
    <statement name="DO">
      <block type="st_image_begin">
        <field name="DEVICE">image_card</field>
        <field name="FORMAT">PGM_GRAY</field>
        <field name="MODE">STREAM</field>
        <value name="PATH">
          <block type="text">
            <field name="TEXT">GRAY.PGM</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="WIDTH">
          <block type="math_number">
            <field name="NUM">32</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="HEIGHT">
          <block type="math_number">
            <field name="NUM">32</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="LEVELS">
          <block type="variables_get">
            <field name="VAR">levels</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="RESERVE">
          <block type="logic_boolean">
            <field name="BOOL">FALSE</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <next>
          <block type="controls_for">
            <field name="VAR">pixel</field>
            <value name="FROM">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">0</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <value name="TO">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">1023</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <value name="BY">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">1</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <statement name="DO">
              <block type="st_image_append_gray">
                <field name="DEVICE">image_card</field>
                <value name="SHADE">
                  <block type="variables_get">
                    <field name="VAR">pixel</field>
                  </block>
                </value>
              </block>
            </statement>
            <next>
              <block type="st_image_finish">
                <field name="DEVICE">image_card</field>
                <next>
                  <block type="controls_if">
                    <value name="IF0">
                      <block type="st_image_ok">
                        <field name="DEVICE">image_card</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <statement name="DO0">
                      <block type="inout_digital_write">
                        <field name="PIN">6</field>
                        <field name="STAT">HIGH</field>
                        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Drive this output LOW or HIGH. This controls the connected digital signal; it does not produce a variable analog voltage.</comment>
                      </block>
                    </statement>
                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Evaluate the condition first. Execute the enclosed actions only when it is true; otherwise skip them or use the else branch.</comment>
                  </block>
                </next>
              </block>
            </next>
            <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Repeat the body with pixel taking each value from the start to the end, using the given step. Array indices start at zero.</comment>
          </block>
        </next>
      </block>
    </statement>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Runs once after power-on or reset. Initialise the program here; repeating measurements and animation belong in loop.</comment>
  </block>
</xml>
